s of the region and that is  that is a general introduction but I  also wanted to introduce specific themes  that are very relevant to people from  the area such as head hunting and  warfare head hunting is something that  seems very alien to western US and well  as yet haunting it it was part of  warfare formerly in in the region but it  also had a very important ritual purpose  it was basically a growing of age  occasion young men had to go out and be  near to prove themselves as young adults  to go out on headhunting raids and also  heads were often taken from from enemy  groups to be part of building village  temples or important ceremonial houses  that is the past we're talking about the  heads of people that's right okay so  this is in the past I also introduced  the the importance and the role of  ancestors in the spirit world and there  is a small display on lifecycle  ceremonies there's also a display that  looks at the earliest surviving forms of  art which are mostly metal work  from going back to or in some cases  3,000 years and these are objects that  show patterns that are still relevant  now if you go to the gallery you may  look out for for example for a little  bronze seated man who's playing a  musical instrument his back is very  elaborately decorated with tattoos now  this figure is from the from the very  early first century BC and right next to  it I display some some stamps that were  used in Borneo to be to be covered with  with black paint and then then stamped  onto the human body as a guide for  making tattoos and they show virtually  the same intricate patterns I think that  is a very interesting connection let's  talk about your role as a curator mm-hmm  you know what kinds of things do you do  perhaps maybe give us a life in the day  of dr. Ruth Barnes well of course I am  first of all responsible for making the  the collection accessible so I am I'm  the person that was responsible for  designing and choosing the objects and  designing the overall display but I did  that very much in collaboration with  with our design team I involved of  course the conservators the our  publication department works closely  with me when it comes to writing labels  and interpreting the material so that it  we make sure that it is actually  accessible specialists are often not the  the best people for  for communicating what they know in  great detail so the wonderful part of  working in the museum for me it's always  that it involves working with so many  different people who have vastly  different talents some of it very hands  on some of it very much intellectual and  academic planning and research focused  and it really makes you feel quite  humble about the different talents that  we all bring to our jobs to work in the  museum and bring it all together into  into something that becomes a creative  and inspiring display right so tell us  what was your goal your ultimate goal in  curating the eggs the installation  because this material is probably quite  new to many of our visitors I really  wanted to in to introduce this  collection or these collections in a way  that is accessible but also makes people  marvel at the ingenuity and the  creativity that speaks through these  objects and what's what is the time span  that the gallery shows pieces from the  earliest material that we display goes  back right to the prehistory of  Southeast Asia these are metal works  bronze objects that go back to the that  have an age of about between three and  four thousand years but the the main the  chronology of the most of our collection  involves the go go's from  on the seventh eighth century AD right  to the present that is studied into the  20th century and in some places early  21st century the there are three main  parts to the collection there is the  large group of of three-dimensional  objects mostly wood sculpture that is in  date I would say most of that is 18 to  the early 20th century then there is a  fabulous collection of textiles which  goes back to an earlier period and our  earliest textiles have i've actually  been able to do some radiocarbon dating  on some of them our earliest textiles go  back to this 15 16th century and then we  have a large collection of about 500  gold objects from central and east java  and that collection dates to the eighth  to the root of the 13th century and so  it has a large chronological range and I  think it is important to have to make  visitors aware of of that time dish and  so I'm very pleased that we able to do  that ok so let me ask you this is now  the exhibit at the Yale gallery the  largest or one of the largest in this  country it certainly is and it also is  of superb quality I think it has very  few rivals in my opinion it really is a  probably the most outstanding collection  of its of its type there are important  collections in elsewhere in the country  but because this is the univer  thirsty museum we also offer the  opportunity of involving students  involving the general public in in  teaching sessions in and we really  invite the the public to make use of our  collections in a way that that enhances  learning and appreciation okay now  moving forward you have pieces that are  in storage now that you will rotate out  at some point yes that affects in  particular the the textiles of course  there's a constant rotation that will  take place and every certainly every six  months we will have new things out  exhales there's a reason i go back  shortly but also eventually i would like  to have a part of the of the gallery  dedicated to more focused in-depth  displays either for a particular from a  particular geographic region or covering  a particular aspect of life that one  finds represented through throughout the  region for one could for example look at  at the consumption of beetle which is a  mixture of it's it's a palm nut arecanut  and the fruit or leap of the siri pepper  which is mixed with lime and is a mil